Bugbot found a real Medium-severity bug on PR #251 (branch `fix/gpg-signature-verification` in `<repo>`), in `internal/pgp/expiry.go`'s `parseColonExpiry` (currently around lines 111-140). Read the full function before changing anything.

**The bug**: the function iterates every `sub:` record in `gpg --with-colons` output that has signing capability (`fields[11]` contains `"s"`), and requires EACH one to have a set, non-expired expiry — if ANY signing-capable subkey is expired, the WHOLE key is rejected:

```go
case "sub":
    if len(fields) < 12 || !strings.Contains(fields[11], "s") {
        continue
    }
    if fields[6] == "" || fields[6] == "0" {
        return fmt.Errorf("key %q has a signing subkey with no expiration date: non-expiring signing keys are not permitted", keyID)
    }
    if err := checkNotExpired(fields[6], now); err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("key %q signing subkey %w", keyID, err)
    }
```

Bugbot's finding: "After a normal subkey rotation the old expired subkey stays on the key for verifying historical signatures, so `CheckKeyExpiry` rejects an identity that still has a valid current signing subkey. This hits the live `Sign` and `DetachSignBody` callers, not just the unwired verifier." This is correct — GPG does not delete old subkeys when a new one is generated (`gpg --quick-add-key`); an owner who has ever rotated their signing subkey will have at least one old, expired signing-capable subkey sitting alongside a valid current one, and the current logic rejects the whole key for that reason alone, even though gpg itself would correctly select and use only the current valid subkey for signing.

**Fix**: change the semantic from "every signing-capable subkey must be valid" to "at least one signing-capable subkey must be currently valid (has an expiry set, and that expiry has not passed)." The security invariant this check exists to enforce (per `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s "Expired and non-expiring keys rejected" invariant and `docs/gpg-signature-verification.md`'s carried-forward criterion on this) is "there must be at least one usable, currently-valid signing key" — not "every historical signing subkey that has ever existed must still be valid," which is not a meaningful security property and actively breaks normal key-rotation hygiene.

Concretely: track whether ANY signing-capable subkey was seen at all, and whether AT LEAST ONE of them is currently valid (has a real, non-past expiry). After the loop:
- If no signing-capable subkey was seen at all, that's fine (some keys have no subkeys at all, or the design's primary-key check already covers the no-subkey case) — the existing pub-only behavior should be unaffected in this case. Confirm this against the existing tests (`TestParseColonExpiry`'s "non-signing subkey without expiry is ignored" case).
- If at least one signing-capable subkey was seen but ALL of them are either missing an expiry or expired, THAT is the rejection case — the key has signing capability delegated to subkeys but none of them are currently usable.
- If at least one signing-capable subkey is currently valid, the key passes on this dimension regardless of how many other expired/no-expiry signing subkeys also exist.

You'll need to restructure the loop to defer the decision until after all `sub:` records are seen (rather than returning immediately from inside the loop on the first bad subkey), since you now need to know about ALL signing-capable subkeys before deciding pass/fail. Be careful this doesn't change the PRIMARY key's own expiry check (the `pub:` case) — that one should stay exactly as strict as it is today (a primary key with no expiry, or an expired primary key, is always a rejection, no "at least one" leniency there — only subkeys get the "at least one valid" treatment, since only subkeys are expected to rotate over a key's lifetime while the primary key does not).

Update the function's doc comment to accurately describe the new semantic. Update or add tests in `internal/pgp/expiry_test.go`'s `TestParseColonExpiry` table: add a case with two signing-capable subkeys, one expired and one currently valid, expecting `wantErr: false` (this is the exact scenario Bugbot described — normal subkey rotation, should pass); and a case with two signing-capable subkeys, BOTH expired, expecting `wantErr: true` (no currently-usable signing subkey at all). Keep the existing "signing subkey without expiration date" case working correctly under the new logic — decide whether a subkey with an empty/zero expiry field counts as "not currently valid" (rejected on its own, but doesn't necessarily reject the whole key if another subkey IS valid) or as an immediate hard-fail regardless of other subkeys; your call, but state and justify the decision in the doc comment, and make sure whichever you choose doesn't contradict the "at least one valid signing subkey" security property.

